-**Comparison boundary.** AEOD is absolute TPR gap, not full equalized odds. Native preserves each procedure’s original prediction rule; raw argmax and shared root-only calibration are parallel outputs of the same checkpoints. The Full replay mixes five CPU and 95 GPU models, with 98 cu128 and two cu130 training builds; all 100 controls use CPU replay and cu128 training in the current driver 595 environment. Historical/current driver equality was not established. Thus these valid-only U and C studies are not a uniform-device final fairness comparison or a completed all-component image ablation. For the separate C20 comparison, Full replay uses two CPU and 18 GPU checkpoints, all 20 minus_C replays use CPU, and both training cohorts use cu128; these counts do not replace the U100 or nine-method historical build disclosures. The nine-seed panel excluding recipe-selection seed 91001 and the six-seed panel retaining 91005–91010 apply the same rules to both procedures; previously exposed validation data do not become untouched confirmation sets by subsetting. The native/shared main endpoint and final evaluation remain for the authors to decide.
+**Additional C-scene trade-offs.** For Sp-DFA, ten-seed paired native/shared ACC/AEOD/ASPD differences are +0.042 ± 0.796 / -0.00668 ± 0.00848 / +0.00810 ± 0.02432, versus +0.097 ± 0.909 / +0.00523 ± 0.01289 / +0.00392 ± 0.01422 in raw, with ACC in percentage points and all values mean ± sample SD (ddof=1). Deletion lowers native/shared AEOD but raises ASPD; raw raises both disparity means. The native/shared ACC difference reverses from +0.134 ± 0.786 for nine seeds to -0.066 ± 0.881 for six seeds, while lower AEOD and higher ASPD persist. For FedSA, deleting C raises native/shared ten-seed ACC and ASPD while lowering AEOD; both gap directions reverse in the fixed nine-/six-seed subsets. Raw nine-seed FedSA improves all three means after deletion, whereas raw six-seed FedSA raises both gaps. S-DFA native/shared deletion lowers ACC and raises both gaps in the ten-seed panel; its ACC direction reverses in the six-seed subset. All unfavorable outcomes and subset reversals remain in the full table, without choosing a favorable view or panel.
